  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nToday...\n\nA narrow mid-level ridge extends from the southern into central \nPlains, between two troughs to the east and west. Weak mid-level \nfrontogenesis, coupled with WAA and low-level moisture transport, is \nsupporting scattered light showers moving east across the area late \nthis morning. This activity should gradually diminish as the ridge \naxis shifts overhead. Some afternoon clearing is possible across \nsoutheast NE and southwest IA, allowing temperatures to peak in the \nlow 70s. Farther north, more persistent clouds and showers will hold \nhighs in the mid 60s. \n\nTonight through Saturday...\n\nThe break in precipitation will be short-lived. Successive \nshortwaves lifting out of the Desert Southwest, coupled with \ncontinued WAA and moisture transport, will support severe rounds of \nshowers through the remainder of the work week. The first round is \nexpected to push in from west to east overnight, with PoPs peaking \nat 60-80% around daybreak Thursday before gradually easing into the \nafternoon and evening. Overall, instability will be rather limited, \nso thunder should be isolated. Intermittent showers will continue \nthrough Friday (PoPs 30-60%), with stronger forcing for ascent \nsupporting another more extensive band of showers Friday night into \nSaturday morning (PoPs 70-90%). \n\nHigh temperatures Thursday and Friday will be confined to the 60s, \nthanks to the ongoing cloud cover and precipitation. Temperatures on \nSaturday will depend on how much clearing occurs behind the \ndeparting precipitation shield. If clouds depart in time to allow \nfor some sunshine into the afternoon, highs reach into the mid 70s, \nwith the highest odds further west. Rainfall totals through the \nperiod are generally expected in the 1-1.50\" range at most \nlocations, though pockets up to 2\" remain possible.\n\nSunday and Beyond...\n\nA brief period of upper-level ridging is expected to move in Sunday \ninto Monday, bringing dry conditions and highs in the low to mid \n70s. Attention then turns to another potentially impactful system \nearly next week. Guidance agrees on the return of showers and \nthunderstorms, but differences in the progression of the upper-level \npattern continues to limit confidence in timing. As the ridge shifts \neast, strengthening southerly flow will transport Gulf moisture \nnorthward into the Plains. Some solutions also suggest a \ncontribution of Pacific tropical moisture associated with the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo, although its eventual influence remains \nuncertain. \n\nA potent, high amplitude shortwave trough is expected to sweep east \nacross the central Plains sometime next week, with long-range \nfavoring late Monday into Wednesday for the passage. With plenty of \nmoisture in place, another widespread round of showers is expected. \nGiven the saturated soil and potential for over-performing moisture \nprofiles, this could support a flooding threat. NBM guidance \ncurrently brings a 60-75% probability of at least 1\", and a 35-45% \nprobability of at least 2\". Additionally, sufficient shear will be \nin place along with some pockets of enhanced instability, bringing \nthe potential for strong to severe storms. GEFS and EPS-based \nmachine learning guidance continued to highlight a 5% probability of \nsevere weather Monday into Tuesday.",
